 "sentences": [
  {
   "start": 0.4,
   "end": 3.13,
   "text": "What if it rained for a hundred years straight?"
  },
  {
   "start": 3.48,
   "end": 4.57,
   "text": "The sky couldn't hold it."
  },
  {
   "start": 4.95,
   "end": 8.94,
   "text": "At any moment, the whole atmosphere carries only about one inch of water."
  },
  {
   "start": 9.33,
   "end": 11.73,
   "text": "That water falls out in about nine days."
  },
  {
   "start": 12.040000000000001,
   "end": 17.09,
   "text": "So for the rain to keep going, the oceans would have to lift it back up, again and again."
  },
  {
   "start": 17.49,
   "end": 22.369999999999997,
   "text": "Earth already does that about forty times a year, which gives roughly three and a half feet of rain."
  },
  {
   "start": 24.22,
   "end": 27.02,
   "text": "Do it for a century, and the same water falls about four thousand times."
  },
  {
   "start": 27.33,
   "end": 31.025,
   "text": "That's around three hundred and forty feet deep, if nothing drained away."
  },
  {
   "start": 33.47,
   "end": 34.04,
   "text": "Rain isn't a supply."
  },
  {
   "start": 34.33,
   "end": 35.5,
   "text": "It's a loop, and the Sun runs it."
  },
  {
   "start": 35.98,
   "end": 38.83,
   "text": "Subscribe so you never miss the next what-if."
  }
 ],
